<td id="A3.T6.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">99.1</td>
<td id="A3.T6.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">80.4</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>External Validity Indices</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For two clusterings U and V, NMI gives us an information-theoretic performance metric, defined as</p>
<table id="A4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle NMI(U,V)=\frac{I(U,V)}{\sqrt{H(U)\cdot H(V)}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle NMI(U,V)=\frac{I(U,V)}{\sqrt{H(U)\cdot H(V)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(U,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(U,V)</annotation></semantics></math> is mutual information and <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(U)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V)</annotation></semantics></math> are the entropies of the clusterings.
ARI gives us a combinatorial way to measure similarity based on the Rand Index (<math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RI" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RI</annotation></semantics></math>) and defined as</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle RI=\frac{(k+l)}{\binom{n}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle RI=\frac{(k+l)}{\binom{n}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ARI=\frac{RI-\mathbb{E}[RI]}{Max(RI)-\mathbb{E}[RI]}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ARI=\frac{RI-\mathbb{E}[RI]}{Max(RI)-\mathbb{E}[RI]}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this last case,
<math id="A4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the number of agreed elements between two clusterings
and
<math id="A4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the number of disagreed elements between two clusterings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Preliminaries ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we also mention ACC as our main external validity index.
ACC provides a replacement for the accuracy in the supervised classification. In order to overcome the labeling issue, we used the Hungarian Algorithm to find the optimal assignment for the clusters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Cluster Validity Indices</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section of our Appendix, we provide the effects of dimension reduction on Silhouette (Sil), Calinski-Harabasz (CHI), and Davies-Bouldin (DBI) indices.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since we are using CVIs to determine the number of clusters, the correlation between these CVIs and our performance evaluation metrics is crucial. Highly correlated results indicate that the maximum/minimum CVIs we obtain also correspond to the maximum/minimum clustering performance</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Silhouette Score</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sil is defined as the following:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Sil}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{\max\{d(i),c(i)\}-d(i)}{c(i)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Sil</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Sil}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{\max\{d(i),c(i)\}-d(i)}{c(i)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="A5.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="A5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A5.I1.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(i)</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">corresponds to the average distance between the point <math id="A5.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and other points in the same cluster.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A5.I1.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(i)</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">corresponds to the average distance between the point <math id="A5.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and points in the nearest cluster.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As it can be seen in Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ E.1 Silhouette Score ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, investigated dimension reduction techniques increase the correlation coefficient between Sil and Acc by more than 10% in the CIFAR-10 dataset.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07184v1/correlation.svg" id="A5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/316;" width="476" height="316"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Effect of Dimension Reduction on the Correlation Coefficients between Silhouette Score and Clustering Accuracy on CIFAR-10 Dataset.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;"></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally to what we present in Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ E.1 Silhouette Score ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we further present Figure <a href="#A5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ E.1 Silhouette Score ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to show the performance of our number of cluster estimation method on a 25-cluster subset of the CIFAR-100 dataset. We apply Normalization + UMAP on this subset and compare our results with the K-means algorithm applied to the original vector space. As it can be seen at the right side of Figure <a href="#A5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ E.1 Silhouette Score ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the highest ACC (94.4%) is also obtained at the highest Sil value, aligning perfectly with the silhouette score whereas the maximum value of the original silhouette score corresponds to a 76.8% accuracy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07184v1/silhouette_umap.svg" id="A5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Effect of the UMAP (3D) method on clustering accuracy and silhouette score on a random CIFAR-25 dataset. Acc is marked at the left, and the Sil is marked at the right axis, respectively. Both plots are aligned according to the Acc values, but the Sil is scaled to match with Acc for demonstration purposes.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Calinski Harabasz Index</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CHI evaluates clustering quality leveraging two matrices called Between-cluster separation matrix (<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Within-cluster dispersion matrix (<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A5.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B_{k}=\sum_{j=1}^{K}n_{j}\,(\mu_{j}-\mu)\,(\mu_{j}-\mu)^{\mathsf{T}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B_{k}=\sum_{j=1}^{K}n_{j}\,(\mu_{j}-\mu)\,(\mu_{j}-\mu)^{\mathsf{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A5.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{k}=\sum_{j=1}^{K}\sum_{x_{i}\in C_{j}}(x_{i}-\mu_{j})\,(x_{i}-\mu_{j})^{\mathsf{T}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{k}=\sum_{j=1}^{K}\sum_{x_{i}\in C_{j}}(x_{i}-\mu_{j})\,(x_{i}-\mu_{j})^{\mathsf{T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="A5.I2" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="A5.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A5.I2.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A5.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">overall centroid of all <math id="A5.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> points.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A5.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A5.I2.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A5.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">centroid of cluster <math id="A5.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A5.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A5.I2.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A5.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">number of points in cluster <math id="A5.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A5.I2.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="A5.I2.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A5.I2.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I2.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">number of clusters.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Using these two matrices one can calculate CHI as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A5.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{CHI}\;=\;\frac{\operatorname{trace}(B_{k})\times(N-K)}{\operatorname{trace}(W_{k})\times(K-1)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>CHI</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>trace</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>trace</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{CHI}\;=\;\frac{\operatorname{trace}(B_{k})\times(N-K)}{\operatorname{trace}(W_{k})\times(K-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ E.2 Calinski Harabasz Index ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows the correlation between CHI and ACC on the CIFAR-10 dataset. The correlation coefficient is -0.69, where there is no dimension reduction technique applied. We observe that Isomap reduction increases this value up to 0.95 ,indicating ACC is highly correlated with CHI.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07184v1/calinski-correlation.svg" id="A5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/253;" width="381" height="253"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Effect of Dimension Reduction on the Correlation Coefficients between Calinski-Harabasz Index and Clustering Accuracy on CIFAR-10 Dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the fact that UMAP (3D) shows a correlation coefficient of 0.76, the increase in the CHI becomes much smaller after the maximum ACC is observed. Figure <a href="#A5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ E.2 Calinski Harabasz Index ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> with the CIFAR-25 dataset also supports our observation on Figure <a href="#A5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ E.2 Calinski Harabasz Index ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07184v1/calinski-isomap.svg" id="A5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Comparison of the UMAP (3D) and Isomap (8D) methods for Calinski-Harabasz Index (CHI) on a randomly chosen CIFAR-25 dataset.
Acc is marked at the left, and CHI is marked at the right axis, respectively. Both plots are aligned according to the Acc values, but CHI is scaled to match with Acc for demonstration purposes.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;"></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Davies-Bouldin Index</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DBI evaluates clustering quality via within‐cluster scatter <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and between‐cluster separation <math id="A5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="A5.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n_{i}}\sum_{x\in C_{i}}\lVert x-\mu_{i}\rVert." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n_{i}}\sum_{x\in C_{i}}\lVert x-\mu_{i}\rVert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Define the cluster‐pair score:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{S_{i}+S_{j}}{\lVert\mu_{i}-\mu_{j}\rVert.}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{S_{i}+S_{j}}{\lVert\mu_{i}-\mu_{j}\rVert.}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the Davies–Bouldin Index is</p>
<table id="A5.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{DBI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>DBI</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{DBI}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}\max_{j\neq i}R_{ij}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K}\max_{j\neq i}R_{ij}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">DBI is another well-known CVI exhibiting promising results to determine the number of clusters. Unlike Sil and CHI, as the smaller DBI becomes, clustering gets better. Figure <a href="#A5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ E.3 Davies-Bouldin Index ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> clearly shows that dimension reduction is crucial for DBI. On the original vector space, Sil behaves much better than both CHI and DBI; however, in a reduced vector space, these CVIs can be used in a cooperative manner.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07184v1/dbi-umap.svg" id="A5.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Effect of the UMAP (3D) method on clustering accuracy and Davies-Bouldin Index (DBI) on a random CIFAR-25 dataset. In DBI, smaller values are better.
Acc is marked at the left, and DBI is marked at the right axis, respectively. Both plots are aligned according to the Acc values, but DBI is scaled to match with Acc for demonstration purposes.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;"></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.4 </span>Average Clustering Coefficient</h3>

<div id="A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike the CVIs we discussed previously, the clustering coefficient is not related to the clustering process, but it is a graph measure that quantifies the clusterability in a network.
That is, the clustering coefficient is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{avg}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{v=1}^{N}C_{v}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{avg}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{v=1}^{N}C_{v}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}=\frac{2\times\text{number of edges between the neighbors of }v}{\text{deg}(v)\times(\text{deg}(v)-1)}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mtext>number of edges between the neighbors of </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>deg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>deg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}=\frac{2\times\text{number of edges between the neighbors of }v}{\text{deg}(v)\times(\text{deg}(v)-1)}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To test the effects on <math id="A5.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{avg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{avg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct a KNN graph using our vector
embeddings and transform the initial vector space into a network clusterability problem. Although the average clustering coefficient does not directly inform us about the clustering performance, it can be used to discuss the intrinsic geometry of our data manifold and the dimensionality of the vector space. High-dimensional spaces tend to be less clusterable due to the curse of dimensionality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Clustering Coefficient and Accuracies for Different Dimension Reduction Techniques on CIFAR-10 dataset.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;"></figcaption>
<table id="A5.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A5.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dimension Reduction</span></th>
<th id="A5.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T7.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Clustering Coefficient</span></th>
<th id="A5.T7.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T7.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A5.T7.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T7.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">None</th>
<td id="A5.T7.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.2955</td>
<td id="A5.T7.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.9100</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T7.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T7.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Normalization + t-SNE (2D)</th>
<td id="A5.T7.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.T7.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.608</span></td>
<td id="A5.T7.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.T7.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">98.5</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T7.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T7.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Normalization + UMAP (3D)</th>
<td id="A5.T7.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.T7.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.562</span></td>
<td id="A5.T7.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.T7.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">98.8</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T7.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T7.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">PCA (30D)</th>
<td id="A5.T7.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.312</td>
<td id="A5.T7.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">90.8</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T7.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T7.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Normalized ISOMAP (32D)</th>
<td id="A5.T7.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.397</td>
<td id="A5.T7.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">97.9</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T7.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T7.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Normalized MDS (24D)</th>
<td id="A5.T7.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.306</td>
<td id="A5.T7.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">98.2</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Average clustering coefficient is not a CVI, but it still carries some information on clusterability. Hence, we also include Table <a href="#A5.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ E.4 Average Clustering Coefficient ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> in this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We made our evaluations by creating KNN graphs with 10 nearest neighbors. Since MDS reduction tries to conserve the original distances between data points, the created KNN graph is ideally the same with the KNN graph created by the original vector space. Thus, for vector spaces reduced with MDS, the average clustering coefficient does not truly represent the clusterability of the data. However, we observe a 100% increase in the clustering coefficient for Normalization + t-SNE (2D) and 90 % increase in Normalization + UMAP (3D) algorithms.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>FasterMSC</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We preferred to make our evaluations with K-Means algorithm in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Dimension Reduction. ‣ 4 Preliminary Investigation on Clustering ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> to present comparable results with previous works throughout the paper. In Table <a href="#A6.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix F FasterMSC ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we further present results obtained by the FasterMSC algorithm on CIFAR-10 dataset. In order to ensure robustness, we initialized the algorithm ten times and accepted the clustering with the highest Sil. FasterMSC results are highly affected by the number of dimensions, and normalization process. In the original vector space, normalization resulted in a 178% increase in ACC. Unlike K-Means algorithm, Normalized PCA reduction increases the performance of FasterMSC by 16% compared to L2 normalization only. Our proposed Normalization + UMAP reduction ends up with 276% increase in ACC compared to the original vector space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Performances of Dimension Reduction Techniques at CIFAR-10 (DINOv2-giant) using <span id="A6.T8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FasterMSC</span> Algorithm.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;"></figcaption>
<table id="A6.T8.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A6.T8.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T8.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="A6.T8.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T8.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ACC</span></th>
<th id="A6.T8.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T8.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NMI</span></th>
<th id="A6.T8.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A6.T8.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ARI</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A6.T8.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">None</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">26.3</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">38.8</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">6.3</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Normalization</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">73.1</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">72.5</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">62.4</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">PCA (30D)</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">26.4</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">36.7</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">5.4</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Normalized PCA</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">85.1</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">90.6</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">84.1</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="A6.T8.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normalization + t-SNE</span></th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A6.T8.4.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">98.5</span></td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A6.T8.4.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">96.6</span></td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A6.T8.4.6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">96.7</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="A6.T8.4.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Normalization + UMAP</span></th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A6.T8.4.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">98.9</span></td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A6.T8.4.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">97.1</span></td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A6.T8.4.7.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">97.6</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">MDS (24D)</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">32.3</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">44.6</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">8.9</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Normalized MDS</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">85.1</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">90.6</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">84.1</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Isomap (32D)</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">74.9</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">87.5</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">75.7</td></tr>
<tr id="A6.T8.4.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A6.T8.4.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Normalized Isomap</th>
<td id="A6.T8.4.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">84.0</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">89.2</td>
<td id="A6.T8.4.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">82.9</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">K-Means is a more robust algorithm than FasterMSC in high-dimensional spaces, but when dimension reduction is applied, FasterMSC results are highly promising. Since FasterMSC maximizes Sil while clustering, it is less sensitive to overestimation of a number of clusters. As it can be seen in Figure <a href="#A6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix F FasterMSC ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, both Sil and ACC of FasterMSC are more stable than results obtained by K-Means. FasterMSC loses less than 4% ACC between 10 and 15 clusters, where this loss is more than 17% for K-Means.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07184v1/fast_vs_k.svg" id="A6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/235;" width="476" height="235"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Performance of FasterMSC and K-Means over Normalization + UMAP (3D) applied CIFAR-10 when initialized with different numbers of clusters.
<br class="ltx_break"></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Additional Results</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a further comparison between our methodology, we also present ACC scores with an unknown number of categories on these datasets in Table <a href="#A7.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix G Additional Results ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. DROWCULA UMAP with an unknown number of categories again performed exemplary better than the prior. Once again, for Tiny ImageNet, the methods presented in Table <a href="#A7.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Appendix G Additional Results ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> set a new SoTA in clustering ACC.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T9" class="ltx_table">
