<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>Bayesian Optimization</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since we increase the correlation between Sil and ACC as it can be seen in Figure <a href="#A5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ E.1 Silhouette Score ‣ Appendix E Cluster Validity Indices ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, applying search algorithms to find the maximum value of Sil becomes applicable.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A7.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ G.1 Bayesian Optimization ‣ Appendix G Additional Results ‣ Dimensionally Reduced Open-World Clustering: DROWCULA" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> clearly shows that Bayesian Optimization can speed up the searching process with a relatively small compromise in ACC. Since the number of iterations for Bayesian Optimization is a hyperparameter, the trade-off between ACC and the number of iterations can be externally determined.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>Effects of Bayesian Optimization across different datasets using DROWCULA UMAP (3D) on normalized data.
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;"></figcaption>
<table id="A7.T10.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
